  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Travel conditions continue to improve for\nholiday travel as surface temperatures go above freezing\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nTravel conditions will continue to improve as we go through the\nevening hours and continue into the weekend. The icing threat\nhas ended from this event but can not rule out localized slick\nspots. \n\nConditions will continue to improve Saturday into Sunday. As no\nimpactful weather is expected during that timeframe. As another\nweather system will begin moving into the area early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another low pressure system is expected to \nimpact the area Monday, though track and impacts remain \nuncertain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nOn Sunday, a deep upper level trough will be centered over the \nOhio Valley and will swing an accompanying low pressure towards \nMaine, strengthen as it tracks across the northeast. The exact \ntrack of the system remains uncertain at this time, but model \nguidance suggests the low will pass somewhere over Maine on \nMonday. Strong winds will be the primary concern with this \nsystem, but the low location will determine how strong the winds\nwill be. The latest forecast has shifted the low slightly \nnorth, and as a result, winds have reduced slightly over waters \nand and along the coast Monday afternoon. If the low is further \nnorth, winds will be weaker, and if the low is more off shore, \nwinds will also be weaker and less onshore. If the low is just \ninland, the southeast winds could produce some coastal flooding \nconcerns. Precipitation will mainly be in the form of rain, with\nsome snow mixing in across the north. The timing and track of \nthe low will become clearer as we get closer to the event.\n\nBehind the cold front, colder air aloft will allow strong winds\naloft to mix down to the surface through the day on Tuesday \nleading to elevated gusts across the area. Again, the timing and\ntrack of the upper level low will determine the strength of \nthese wind gusts possible solutions will become clearer as we \nget closer to the event.",
